lesson physics-magnets v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=4 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Magnets"
  goal "Say which things a magnet pulls, predict what happens when two magnets meet, and follow the poles along a row of magnets."

  look {
    scene 22x20 {
      column two at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        magnet things mode=things count=4 sticks=2
        magnet facing mode=poles same=1
      }
    }
    say """
      A magnet pulls iron and steel and nothing else, so it leaves wood, plastic and glass alone.
      Every magnet has two ends, a north and a south. Two ends that are different pull together.
      Two ends that are the same push apart.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.magnet-count count=2 seed=9
    practice physics.poles count=2 seed=1
    practice physics.poles-predict count=2 seed=2
    practice physics.magnet-picks count=2 seed=3
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.metal-not-magnetic f=0 x="nail" y="coin" z="ruler" w="eraser"
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.poles-chain e=0 x=0 y=1 easy-y=0 hard-x=1
  }
  remember {
    say "Different ends pull together. The same ends push apart."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Fridge magnets and a handful of household objects are enough. A coin is the interesting one:
    dollar coins are not magnetic, which surprises children who expect all metal to stick. The row
    of three magnets at the end needs one more fact the lesson only states: a magnet's two ends are
    always different. Two bar magnets and a pencil mark on each end make it easy to check.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the magnet's things are few, the tray holds no metal a magnet leaves alone, and the chain of magnets pulls at every join."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the magnet's things are five or six, the tray holds a coin and foil that are metal but not magnetic, and the chain of magnets pushes at every join."
  }
}
